= Flash memory =
== Overview ==
{{:Flash memory/Simple}}
'''Flash memory''' is electrically programmable and erasable '''nonvolatile semiconductor memory'''. It represents stored information using engineered charge-storage states, commonly floating-gate or charge-trap devices. Unlike [[Computer architecture|working-memory]] DRAM, it retains information without continuous refresh, but its program, erase and read operations have different granularities and reliability constraints.<ref name="history">Kioxia, [https://www.kioxia.com/en-jp/rd/technology/history.html Technology Development History], corporate record of Toshiba flash developments in 1984 and NAND in 1987; historical priority is attributed to the manufacturer.</ref>
== NAND and NOR ==
'''NAND flash''' organizes memory cells in strings for high-density storage and normally uses page-based programming and block-based erase. '''NOR flash''' supports addressable reads suited to code storage and memory-mapped access in appropriate devices. These are broad architectural tendencies, not universal performance rankings; specific interfaces and devices determine what operations are supported.
== Physical state and endurance ==
Programming and erasure change stored charge and therefore a transistor's threshold-voltage distribution. Finite oxide endurance, charge leakage, read/program disturb, temperature and manufacturing variability can affect data reliability. Multiple bits per cell require narrower voltage-state margins and more sophisticated sensing and error management. A power interruption during programming is not equivalent to loss of all stored charge.
== Device versus managed storage ==
Raw NAND exposes physical program/erase constraints; a managed device may add a flash translation layer, error-correcting codes, bad-block management, wear leveling and garbage collection. An SSD's logical writes and durability promises depend on its controller, interface, firmware, cache and power-failure handling, not solely on nonvolatile cells.<ref name="onfi">Open NAND Flash Interface Workgroup, [https://onfi.org/files/ONFI_6_0_Final.pdf ''Open NAND Flash Interface Specification'', revision 6.0, 18 December 2025], NAND interface and command contracts; not a universal NOR or SSD specification.</ref>
== Historical and specification boundaries ==
Kioxia attributes the flash concept and 1984 announcement to Toshiba's work, followed by NAND development in 1987. ONFI revision 6.0 specifies one interoperable NAND interface family; it is not the definition of every flash product, historical revision or storage protocol.
== Connections ==
[[Related to::Computer architecture|Computer architecture]] uses persistent storage alongside volatile memory; [[Related to::Semiconductor|semiconductor]] devices supply the charge-storage mechanism; [[Related to::Operating system|operating systems]] coordinate persistence through higher-level software. The pending [[Computer memory]] review PR #96 covers memory hierarchy broadly, whereas this article treats flash-device operation and reliability.
